\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Not Justified -  \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Governor\b0\par\par\uc2 1,243 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 2010\par 19:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Robert Flint, 212-416-2216; \par robert.flint@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1810 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite the strength the Chilean peso, current conditions don't justify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market,\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jose De Gregorio told Chile's Senate. The peso has been trading most of the fourth quarter at around 31-month highs against the dollar, which exporters claim is cutting the competitiveness of their products abroad. De Gregorio said that while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  couldn't be ruled out, it is an expensive and not always useful tool to weaken the exchange rate. He added that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market could have negative consequences on the economy and could fuel capital inflows that would be counterproductive to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (carolina.pica@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (robert.flint@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 17:52 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD completely pared the gains it made this morning in volatile trading ahead of the 2:15 p.m. EST FOMC statement. USD now slightly down on day. USD declined overnight against most major rivals as traders braced for further word from Fed on QE. USD has been down for months on expectations the Fed would embark on more Treasury purchases meant to revive stagnant US economy. Fed unlikely to deviate from QE plan even though economy shows further signs of recovery, say analysts. EUR/USD at 1.3418 from 1.3393 late Mon, according to EBS via CQG. USD/JPY at 83.34 from 83.39.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  David Biderman, 212-416-4437; \par david.biderman@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1747 GMT [Dow Jones] The ruble will post steady gains next year amid expected monetary tightening by Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , waning capital outflows, an upcoming Russian eurobond and a growing current account surplus, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is its "highest conviction" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pick for the year. "The underperformance of the ruble versus other emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has left the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at an attractive technical entry point for medium-term trades," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  adds. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 17:40 GMT [Dow Jones] Nearest 1-yr calendar spread is widening before the FOMC announcement this afternoon, reflecting market views that short-term rates will move higher at a speedier pace. Mar 2012 contract prices interest rates 64.5 BP above rate priced into Mar '11 contract. Same spread at 60 BP Mon and as tight as 32.5 BP as recently as the Dec. 6 settlement. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  David Biderman, 212-416-4437; \par david.biderman@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1729 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CHF down 1% Tue, with EUR/CHF not far behind, trading off 0.8%. "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  most on the outs right now are the USD and EUR," says Brian Dolan of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .com. That is because of worries about QE2 in the US and eur-zone sovereign debt concerns, he says. The Swiss franc is a traditional haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that many investors regard as a safe bet. At the same time, commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like CAD and AUD are trading up Tuesday as investors dive into higher yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he says. In early afternoon trade, USD/CAD reaches intraday low of 1.0028.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1720 GMT [Dow Jones] A barbell investment strategy of being short a basket of emerging market bonds and long a basket of developed market bonds makes perfect sense, says David Rosenberg, chief economist and strategist at Gluskin/Sheff in Toronto. Rosenberg notes that inflation data from Europe is subdued despite rallying commodities. He cites this observation from a speech Mon by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  Governor Carney: "if nominal exchange rates do not change, the adjustment will come through inflation in emerging economies and disinflation in major advanced economies." (don.curren@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1653 GMT [Dow Jones] Investment-grade and high-yield credit spreads will tighten further in 2011, according to a BofA Merrill Lynch Global Research report. They say liquidity in the credit markets should continue to drive returns and forecast "around a 10% return on U.S. high-yield bonds and around 7% return on emerging market corporate bonds." High-grade bonds won't fare so well as BofA expects returns of 2.5% in 2011 - significantly lower than those in recent years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (kellie.geressy@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1648 GMT [Dow Jones] Fixed income returns will fall to "low single digits" in 2011 according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jeffrey Rosenberg\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of Global Credit Strategy and US Fixed Income at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 . He believes that "relative to the past two years of double-digit fixed income returns, next year marks a significant decline in return expectations," noting that IG corporate bonds will post "modest positive returns in the intermediate-term maturity range, but returns from Treasuries could be negative." He added that "with the Fed on hold and continued demand from mutual funds and foreigners, fixed-income markets are not expected to suffer big losses" but expects that yields should continue to rise modestly in 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (kellie.geressy@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1646 GMT [Dow Jones] BofA \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees 10-year Treasury ending 2011 at 4% and 2-yr ending 2011 at 1.25%, from current 3.383% and 0.625%. As for near-term trading ranges, Priya Misra says 3% to 3.50% for 10-yr Tsy, with fair value at 3%. Tsy yield jump of late has not really been driven by fundamentals, she says, with last 25bp move about adjusting trading positions given a huge position overhang. Misra noted that ylds shot up by some 70bp last December only to quickly reverse in Jan. Regarding Fed policy, Misra says QE3 is unlikely. (deborah.blumberg@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1643 GMT [Dow Jones] BofA \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees inflation rising over next few months in China, with pressures coming from food and vegetable prices, continuing the trend seen in the second half of 2010. The bank sees China raising rates by 75 bps in three shots of 25 bps next year, as well as gradual appreciation of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The dollar is forecast to trade at CNY6.40 by the end of 2011, compared with about CNY6.66 Tuesday. Chinese growth will slow next year, the bank said, though not significantly. BofA Merrill thinks \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will gradually converge to fair value. Bank economist Ethan Harris says China is a warning signal that "we need ongoing tightening in emerging markets even as Fed and ECB stay on hold."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (deborah.blumberg@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1636 GMT [Dow Jones]Treasurys just recently hit fresh session lows. The benchmark 10-yr yield touches 3.397%, breaking above the 3.393% set Monday. It is the highest level since June 3 and next key level is 3.5%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1635 GMT [Dow Jones] Canadian bonds are lower along with Tsys and are pacing that market in most maturities. One exception is the two-year bond, which is lagging Tsys. Solid US data appear to be the key driver ahead of the FOMC statement this afternoon. The Canadian two-year bond is yielding 1.712% vs 1.671% late Mon. The 10-year yield is at 3.338% vs 3.230%, while 30-years are at 3.741% vs 3.656%. (don.curren@dowjones.com) [ 12-14-10 1310ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101214e6ce000ch\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
