\par\par \b \uc2 Stiglitz: US Should Consider Implementing 2nd Stimulus Package\b0\par\par\uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2010\par 18:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The U.S. should consider a second stimulus package to prop up its struggling economy, Nobel laureate economist Joseph Stiglitz said Friday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. gross domestic product will likely increase between 3.0% and 3.6% in 2011 year-on-year, after rising by around 2.5% this year.\par \par \uc2 "The first stimulus package had too much emphasis on tax cuts. Those were relatively ineffective and not enough aid for the states," Stiglitz told reporters on the sidelines of a seminar in Chile's capital.\par \par \uc2 A new stimulus package should include a revenue-sharing program to make up for a shortfall in state revenue and should pick up the states' investments that had to be stalled. Also, there should be a special focus on human capital, in particular on education and training, he said.\par \par \uc2 "We have to believe that the economy will eventually recover...[W]hat kinds of jobs will we want to have in five years...[W]e need to have people trained for that," Stiglitz said.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, Stiglitz argued the U.S. Federal Reserve's quantitative easing bond-purchasing program is creating an excess of liquidity, which is flowing into emerging-market nations.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market economies such as Chile's are growing at a much faster pace than the U.S. and have comparatively higher interest rates, making them attractive destinations for investors looking for higher returns.\par \par \uc2 According to Stiglitz, the $600 billion bond-purchasing program has created a large amount of "liquidity looking for relatively safe high returns" that aren't found in the U.S. but can be found in many emerging-market nations.\par \par \uc2 As many emerging markets are trying to discourage capital inflows, "the liquidity goes to the places where they haven't yet put barriers for the inflows," Stiglitz told reporters.\par \par \uc2 As the Columbia University professor and 2001 co-winner of the Nobel price for Economics sees it, this increasing pressure will likely lead those countries that haven't yet \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets or imposed capital controls to eventually do so.\par \par \uc2 "These countries that are open would be better advised to set up a framework of controls with a willingness to adjust the intensity [of inflows] depending on the pressure they feel," he said.\par \par \uc2 Chile, one of the remaining Latin American nations that hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, has recently seen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surge to 31-month highs against the dollar as international copper prices, a bellwether for the nation's economy, surged to record highs.\par \par \uc2 The Andean nation "should be ready to do a whole portfolio [of measures], because none of them are perfectly effective...They should be ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in exchange rates," said Stiglitz.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's former reserve requirement, which effectively "was a tax on short-term flows, but encouraged long-term flows...was a pretty good system," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has maintained that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a tool that's always available, but it will only use it if the exchange rate, in real terms, moves out of line with its long-term fundamentals. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says that despite the peso's recent appreciation, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still in line with fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Exporters in the South American nation have clamored for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , arguing the peso's strength is eating away at the competitiveness of their products.\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 10-12-10 1756GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101210e6ca001e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
