\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Inflows may pressure emerging markets-Stiglitz\b0\par\par\uc2 304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2010\par 17:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Stiglitz says second round of U.S. stimulus needed\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso, Stiglitz says (Updates with fresh Stiglitz comments)\par \par \uc2 By Alonso Soto and Moises Avila\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Dec 10 (Reuters) - Smaller developing countries should prepare measures to be able to cope with inflows of cash as investors look for bigger returns outside sluggish developed economies, Nobel Prize winning economist Joseph Stiglitz said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Stiglitz told a conference in Chile that he expected the Europe and the United States recovery to be "very slow," and that Washington should roll out a second round of stimulus.\par \par \uc2 He said there is a chance the euro could fail and that its sustainability would depend on a fiscal framework that helps struggling European nations like debt-stricken Ireland.\par \par \uc2 Given the relative vigor of some emerging market economies, their governments may have to resort to steps to curb an overdose of inward investment, he said.\par \par \uc2 Chile, which has refrained from capital controls, should be ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local peso <CLP=CL> that is trading at around 30-month highs. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is boosted by rising dollar inflows and soaring prices for No.1 export copper. For more see [ID:nN10134458].\par \par \uc2 "They (\b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) should also be ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange rate," said Stiglitz, who won a Nobel Prize in economics in 2001. "It depends on what is going elsewhere in the world, the magnitude of the (inflows) pressure."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter peso strength in 2008, when it hit around 430 per dollar. The peso was trading at around 475.50 pesos a dollar in late Friday trade. (Writing by Simon Gardner. Editing by W. Simon)\par \par \uc2 USA-ECONOMY/STIGLITZ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101210e6ca0017s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
