\par\par \b \uc2 Chile's Peso Closes Weaker On Falling Euro, Copper Prices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2010\par 18:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso closed weaker against the dollar Monday as the euro lost versus the dollar on flaring concerns about euro-zone debt and as international copper prices slipped on fears China will again tighten its monetary policy to slow its surging economy.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP482.40 to the dollar, versus Friday's close of CLP481.20, while trading in a range of CLP481.70 to CLP485.10.\par \par \uc2 As Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and a third of its exports are bound for Europe, the peso often moves in the same direction the euro does against the dollar. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped against the dollar as worries about the region's crisis of sovereign debt grew on speculation Ireland might soon request a European Union lifeline.\par \par \uc2 Also, as Chile is the world's premier copper producer, accounting for roughly a third of global supply, the peso often takes trading cues from the metal's international prices. Spot copper in London fell 1.4% to $3.90 a pound, according to Chile's state copper commission Cochilco, as a move by China to further tighten monetary policy could hurt demand for the metal from the world's largest consumer.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, exporters in \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  have clamored for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the peso, recently near a 30-month high, hurts the competitiveness of their products.\par \par \uc2 "The competitiveness of our non-copper exports is at risk. Especially in the case of manufactured [products], whose main export destination is the U.S.," said Eduardo Moyano, president of Chile's Asexma exporters trade group\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed after heavy buying of the shorter bonds by the nation's private pension fund managers.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.66%, from 2.68% on Friday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 3.06%, from 3.05% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 15-11-10 1723GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101115e6bf001bw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
