\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: As  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Peso Gains, Risk Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Nears\b0\par\par\uc2 1,281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2010\par 20:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1915 GMT [Dow Jones] As Chile's peso continues to strengthen versus the dollar --it was recently approaching a 30-month high-- an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market becomes more likely. "The real exchange rate is getting closer to levels it was at during the last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2008," investment bank BCI said. Unlike the last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , inflation expectations are now within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's "range of tolerance", which would allow the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  some leeway in hiking interest rates. "[This] is a necessary factor so that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is consistent and it increases its chances of being successful," BCI said. Peso ended weaker Monday at CLP480.10 to dollar, versus CLP477.40 on Friday. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1912 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite strong 3-yr sale, Treasurys are now falling broadly as investors prepare for 10-yr sale Tuesday and 30-yr auction Wednesday. "We've got more supply coming up and supply is the key focus of the week," says Mary Ann Hurley, at \uc2 D.A. Davidson & Co\uc2 . But she expects the 10-year auction will go okay as the Fed QE2 is bond friendly. The 30-yr sale could be more problematic given that it is largely ignored by the Fed's $600 bln shopping spree between now and June, she says. 10-year note is 6/32 lower to yield 2.561%.\par \par \uc2 (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1906 GMT [Dow Jones]Having started a corrective move lower, NZD/USD may well continue towards 0.7700 territory assisted by weakness in EUR, AUD, says ANZ in its morning brief; "A continued focus on offshore movements should see the NZD topside limited again today." Pair last 0.7885 vs 0.7890 late yesterday. Adds markets now focused on euro-zone; "After being fixated with QE for some time, having taken delivery of it, markets quickly tired of it, and with no major data out overnight, attention (is) shifting back to that other trusty old chestnut, European sovereign debt." Tips range of 0.7833-0.7903. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1905 GMT [Dow Jones] International Securities Exchange says it's exploring "new business opportunities" for its technology subsidiary Longitude, including in the "parimutuel wagering industry." One idea on the table -- modernize the centuries-old sport of horse racing, which could see ISE's Longitude help create a single-pool wagering system for New Jersey's equestrian industry that would make the business more efficient and transparent, ISE says. Dennis Dowd, consultant for Longitude, testified on the topic today before a committee of NJ's state senate. "Many parties, including horsemen, patrons and multiple \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and technology providers, would have the opportunity to participate in and benefit from single pool wagering," companies say in a statement. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1858 GMT [Dow Jones] Dallas Fed's Fisher (non-voter) says the recently announced fresh round of asset purchases--dubbed quantitative easing--was "risky business" that could become the "new normal" of monetary policy, with investors expecting a steady drip of cheap Fed money that could lead to spiraling inflation. "The Fed is doing its level best to deliver on the dual mandate it was given by the Congress," said Fisher, referring to the Fed's mandates on employment and inflation. "But monetary accommodation, by itself, is not the answer to our current woes," Fisher said in prepared text. Uncertainty over fiscal and regulatory policy out of Washington is keeping businesses from hiring new employees, Fisher said, echoing what he has been saying for most of the year. (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1844 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  expects the Netherlands' auction of EUR2.5 billion-to-EUR3.5 billion worth of 3.50% July 2020 bonds to go well. That's because it will be the last on-the-run [benchmark] bond auction this year and also because the bond is expected to gain some support as the flight-to-quality bid in Europe persists, says strategist Nishay Patel. He says the bond on offer has cheapened sharply versus its comparable prior bond, the 10-year 4% July 2019 over the past two weeks. Citi remains overweight Netherlands within the core euro zone markets. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1835 GMT [Dow Jones] St Louis Fed's Bullard (voter) says he expects consumption and investment should rise from bond buying, but the Fed cannot fix all of the economy's woes. He says Fed could pause buying and sell assets if economy improves. Seems we're far from that spot, although low for long is allowing large chunks of the Fed's mortgage portfolio to run off smoothly, and that's a much better scenario than whacking the MBS market. Treasurys soften a bit, 10-yr off 4/32 to 2.55%. (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1832 GMT [Dow Jones] Auto sector bonds continue to emerge as demand for them has been "fairly strong," said Michael Collins, senior investment officer at Prudential Fixed Income. "Auto sales are picking up and auto financing has also picked up." he expects strong supply for the rest of the year until mid-December. Investors interested in buying these bonds are those who are "looking for high quality assets with reasonable spreads over Libor." (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1831 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed's "low for long plan" really hurts savers. Average yields on consumer deposits, from three-month to five-year CDs, dip below 1% for the first time since 1990 and possibly since the early 1950s, according to Market Rates Insight. Monday it hit 0.98%, and that includes special CDs, which banks use to target new investors and typically yield 40BP or 0.4%, more than conventional CDs, said Dan Geller, executive vp at MRI. The average across different deposit accounts, including checking, savings, money market accounts and CDs, already dipped below 1% in July, but CDs were the last to reach these lows individually. It's all part of the Fed plan to get us to spend.(katy.burne@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1821 GMT [Dow Jones] Mortgage-backeds are off by a tick or two, following Treasurys in a very firm spread environment. Prepayments, which, under normal circumstances, would be going through the roof with rates at these levels, are causing no panic here. Analysts do not expect to see a real jump, especially as some firms have had to redirect origination staff to deal with foreclosure issues. Even the superpremiums (backed by 5-6% loans) are coming in very slowly, and provide a nice yield pick up to Treasurys. (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1813 GMT [Dow Jones] Long-term Treasury futures prices about unchanged after solid $32 bln 3-yr Tsy note auction, reflecting conflicting market forces of more supply this week and Fed QE Tsy purchases. Dec 10-yr Tsy note futures recently 1+/32 higher at 127-15. Dec 30-yr Tsy bond futures recently flat at 130-22. Both contracts remain inside Fri's trading ranges. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1813 GMT [Dow Jones] CAD has strengthened in early afternoon trading in tandem with EUR. It mad a brief foray above parity, pushing USD to a session low at 0.9993, but has since ceded some of its gains and is currently around 1.0020. Analysts generally remain favorable on CAD, but at the moment the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is drifting as traders look for fresh incentives for another sustained assault on parity. (don.curren@dowjones.com) [ 11-08-10 1415ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101108e6b8000ck\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
