\par\par \b \uc2 Chile FinMin would prefer 'somewhat' weaker peso\b0\par\par\uc2 487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2010\par 16:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Finance minister wants weaker peso\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been weighing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Peso near 2-1/2 year highs\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Reuters) - Finance Minister Felipe Larrain would like Chile's peso, which has sharply appreciated in recent weeks to near 2-1/2 year highs, to weaken, hinting in an interview published on Sunday he believed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a possibility.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso has soared around 14 percent since the end of June, prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  warnings from Chilean authorities concerned about the competitiveness of local exporters.\par \par \uc2 "If you ask my opinion, I would prefer the exchange rate to be somewhat higher (peso to be somewhat weaker) than it is today," Larrain told La Tercera newspaper's Sunday edition, reiterating the peso's gains were cause for worry.\par \par \uc2 "I have to respect what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides (on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) -- they have the last word," he added. "But no-one ever said that in a floating exchange rate regime that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was impossible."\par \par \uc2 Larrain said the upward pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso would last as long as the United States' economy remained weak.\par \par \uc2 "There is no increase in U.S. rates looming a reasonable horizon to make financial flows towards the U.S. economy attractive, and as long as China does not move its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we will have this pressure," he added.\par \par \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday it would gradually raise foreign investment limits for pension funds to 80 percent from 60 percent as it seeks to counter the recent sharp gains of the peso.\par \par \uc2 The bank said the limits on the pension system, which manages more than $138 billion, would begin rising in December, with three more increases coming in the following nine months. Greater investment abroad would increase demand for dollars, helping to weaken the peso or at least offset capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's unveiling of new monetary stimulus on Wednesday had added pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Larrain said.\par \par \uc2 Chile has so far held off on buying dollars in the local market, like Colombia, or raising taxes on foreign investment in local assets, as Brazil did twice last month.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it constantly weighs direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but fundamentals do not justify action at the peso's current levels.\par \par \uc2 The bank cited the peso's strength when it slowed the pace of increases to its benchmark interest rate last month. Higher interest rates have attracted foreign investors in recent months, adding to the peso's appreciation.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came in 2008, when the peso was trading around 430 per dollar and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began a program of dollar purchases to increase international reserves and weaken the peso. The peso closed on Friday at 477.30/477.80 per dollar. (Writing by Simon Gardner, editing by Maureen Bavdek)\par \par \uc2 CHILE-ECONOMY/LARRAIN|LANGEN|Table|