\par\par \b \uc2 Chile's Peso Closes 1.9% Stronger On Reaction To US Fed Decision\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 516 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 17:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's peso ended 1.9% stronger against the dollar Thursday as market participants reacted to the Federal Reserve's decision to pump $600 billion into the ailing U.S. economy between now and the middle of next year.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's decision was announced after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closed Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP479.90 to the dollar, nearing a 30-month high, versus CLP489.10 a session earlier. It traded in a wide range of CLP479.20 to CLP484.50.\par \par \uc2 As Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and a third of its exports go to Europe, the peso often moves in the same direction as the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does against the dollar. Investors fled the dollar, sending it sharply lower versus the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the Fed's bond-buying plan.\par \par \uc2 Since Chile is the world's premier copper producer, accounting for a third of global supply, the peso also often takes trading cues from the metal's international prices. Copper futures in New York rallied 3% as the dollar weakened internationally.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to gradually increase the cap on private pension fund managers' investments abroad to 80% from the current 60% -- which was announced earlier in the day and is seen as part of an effort to weaken the peso -- had a limited effect on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The pension fund managers, known locally as AFPs, are the nation's largest institutional investors and had the equivalent of 45.6% of their total assets invested abroad --despite the 60% cap-- as of Sept. 30, according to the quarterly data released by the SAFP pension fund regulator.\par \par \uc2 "The effect on the exchange rate will be limited, because the AFPs already had plenty of room to invest abroad before reaching their current investment limit," said Patricio Roncagliolo, director at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader FXCM \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Exporters have been clamoring for market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength eats away at the competitiveness of their products.\par \par \uc2 "The move shows, however, that the authorities are keeping a watch on the exchange rate," he added.\par \par \uc2 For his part, Finance Minister Felipe Larrain noted the measure will "balance capital flows and that will tend to impact the exchange rate in the medium term," according to local newswire Valor Futuro.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower as the local fixed-income market took cues from yields on U.S. Treasurys, which also fell following the Fed's announcement.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.65%, from 2.68% on Wednesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 3.06%, from 3.13% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Carolina Pica contributed to this article.) [ 04-11-10 1701GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101104e6b4001mo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
