\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1- \highlight22\uc2 Chile fx intervention \highlight\uc2  nearer, peso slows rate-Cbank\b0\par\par\uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2010\par 17:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Stronger peso weighed on Oct. rate hike - minutes\par \par \uc2 * Cenbank says would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in peso if justified\par \par \uc2 * Unemployment rate lowest since early 2009\par \par \uc2 By Antonio de la Jara and Brad Haynes\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciating peso has made \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  more likely than it was three months ago and slowed the pace of interest rate hikes, according to an interview with the bank president and bank meeting minutes published Friday.\par \par \uc2 Chile's surging economic activity would suggest sharper interest rate hikes, but the peso's appreciation, slower inflation and weakness in developed economies point to a more gradual pace of rate hikes, minutes from the bank's October policy meeting showed.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated around 12 percent since the end of June. Exporters impacted by the strong peso have called on authorities to step in, but in an interview published on Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jose De Gregorio said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not "justified" at the exchange rate's current levels.\par \par \uc2 "We have to monitor, but undoubtedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more probable today than it was three months ago," De Gregorio told leading financial newspaper Diario Financiero. "If \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were justified, we'd be doing it."if capital inflows become heavy, but he said that is not now the case.\par \par \uc2 "We don't have a massive net inflow of capital. We have a small deficit deficit in the current account this year," De Gregorio said.\par \par \uc2 The continued appreciation of the peso may also reduce inflationary pressures on Chile's small open economy, Friday's bank minutes suggested.\par \par \uc2 "Our main takeaway is that the (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is very close to ending the hiking cycle that started in June," said Benito Berber, an economist with Nomura Securities. "In fact, we now believe that the (bank) will increase the policy rate by an additional 25 bps and then it will call it quits."\par \par \uc2 De Gregorio told Diario Financiero October's 25 basis-point rate hike did not represent a trend, citing the continued dynamism of Chile's economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Chile's jobless rate reached its lowest level in a year and a half, government data showed on Friday, as the July-September jobless rate fell to 8.0 percent, below market forecasts and down from 8.3 percent in the June-August period.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters had forecast an unemployment rate of 8.1 percent for July to September, as the economy consolidates its recovery from the global economic crisis and a massive February earthquake.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its benchmark interest rate to a record low 0.5 percent amid the financial crisis and kept it there following the devastating quake, before joining a regional rate tightening cycle in June.\par \par \uc2 Now the strong peso, bolstered by Chile's surging economy and high prices of top export copper, is contributing to a slower withdrawal of monetary stimulus, according to minutes from the bank's last meeting.\par \par \uc2 The minutes showed all board members voted to raise rates by 25 basis points to 2.75 percent during October's meeting, although the bank also considered raising rates by 50 basis points as it had done the previous four months.\par \par \uc2 "Under any of the options considered, the benchmark rate would still be expansive, so in future meetings it will probably be necessary to continue withdrawing monetary stimulus," the minutes said. (Editing by Simon Gardner and Andrew Hay)\par \par \uc2 CHILE-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|FUN    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101029e6at00287\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
