\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Exporters Group Asks For  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,255 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2010\par 17:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1522 GMT [Dow Jones] Chile's Asexma exporters trade group called on the government to "use all the available tools in the market" to help the export sector as the peso's strong appreciation hurts the competitiveness of their products. Among other things, Asexma is asking the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or finance ministry to start buying "large sums" of dollars on a daily basis. Earlier this month, the peso firmed to a 29-month high versus the dollar, but a recent barrage of verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and government have moderated its strength. On Wednesday the peso was weaker at CLP495.50 to the dollar, versus Tuesday's close of CLP491.30. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1519 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys recoup some losses and are now off session lows. The decline in US stocks are spurring money flowing back into bonds. Higher yields also lure bargain hunters. 10-year note is 9/32 lower to yield 2.678%. DJIA off 131. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1517 GMT [Dow Jones] Muni prices are down as much as 6 bps in the longer end of the curve as heavy supply and Treasury weakness weigh. New muni deals are seeing "lethargic" demand, and they must be priced with concessions to move, says \uc2 Miller Tabak\uc2 's Michael Pietronico. Also, market is getting "defensive" before the Fed's widely anticipated decision next week to buy more Treasurys, and there's disappointment the purchases may be smaller than earlier hopes, he says. Still, the muni price moves "should ultimately help the market," as yields had gone so low.(romy.varghese@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1514 GMT [Dow Jones] Pimco's Bill Gross is out with his monthly musing on the market, and the news isn't good for Treasury bulls. Gross says the Fed's announcement of a renewed commitment to QE has been well telegraphed and the market's reaction is likely to be subdued. Furthermore, the Fed's announcement will "likely signify the end of a great 30-year bull market in bonds and the necessity for bond managers and, yes, equity managers to adjust to a new environment," he says. Pimco already cut US government-related holdings, a codeword for Treasurys, over the past few months. Pimco has scooped up other relatively safe assets with higher yields -- agency MBS and high-grade corporate bonds. Pimco has also snapped up TIPS to hedge against runaway inflation in the longer term due to an accommodative Fed. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1513 GMT [Dow Jones] Price guidance for \uc2 Travelers Cos Inc\uc2 . (TRV) $1 billion note offer has a 10-yr tranche in the area of 120-125 bps over Treasurys and a 30-year tranche at 135-140 over. Tranche sizes TBD. The sr fixed-rate bonds are expected to be rated A2 by \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , A- by S&P and A by Fitch. Proceeds will be used for general corporate purposes and to fund part of a tender offer for 6.25% junior debentures. The bonds are expected to price this afternoon via \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 , \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  and \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 . (michael.aneiro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1512 GMT [Dow Jones] South Africa's proposed increased reserve accumulation and looming exchange control liberalization highlighted in the medium-term budget policy statement is mildly rand-bearish, says Standard Bank. It says added to this the door is still open for another interest rate cut in light of a benign inflation backdrop. USD/ZAR currently trades at 7.0691. (robb.stewart@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1506 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD dropping further, hitting intraday low of 1.3762 from 1.3855 late Tue, according to EBS via CQG, as US stocks sag, also hitting intraday lows, with DJIA down nearly 1.25%. USD piling on earlier gains on the back of pared expectations for Fed's QE program.\par \par \uc2 (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1459 GMT [Dow Jones] Argentina's bonds are rallying Wed, following the death of former president--and husband to the current president--Nestor Carlos Kirchner. "It's analogous to a stock rallying 5 percent when a bad CEO leaves/dies," said Sara Zervos, an international debt portfolio manager at Oppenheimer. While his death will surely be mourned, Zervos adds: "Things will be messy as there will be a power struggle. But long run the market (and my analysts and I) see this as the first clear opportunity for significant, positive political change in Argentina." (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1447 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts extend losses Wednesday, pushing yields to their highest since August on supply related hedging and general weakness in core government bonds. Gilts had already fallen sharply on Tuesday as hopes of BOE reviving asset purchases at next week's meeting fizzled out after a faster than expected rise in UK 3Q GDP. The underperformance continues Wednesday, with 10-year gilt/bund yield spread widening 3bps to 58.3bps. "The bigger fall in gilts is due to combination of the gilt syndication, with players selling the 10-year futures contract, and the troubles in the euro zone periphery, which helped bunds a bit," says Matteo Regesta at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . UK priced GBP5Bln UKT 2040 at 2.5bps over UKT 2039 in the last conventional syndication of the year. December gilts are down 0.97 at 122.53 and the 10-year gilt yield climbs to 3.148%, having risen to 3.198% earlier Wednesday, the highest since August 11. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1438 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungarian government bonds close weaker once again as foreign investors' confidence in Hungary is fading due to the government's latest plan to curb the constitutional court's power. Bond yields move up 5-10bps; 3-year benchmark bond yield is 6.70%, 5-year is 6.90%, 10-year is 7.10%. Bonds have been easing since the beginning of the week despite a steady EUR/HUF rate, one trader says, adding "there's no sign of demand for Hungarian government bonds." EUR/HUF is at 275.07 from 274.73 in the morning. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1433 GMT [Dow Jones] The death of former Argentine president Nestor Kirchner "introduces significant political uncertainty as the pro-government political forces are likely to realign significantly ahead of next year's presidential elections," says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  in a research note. "At face value, President Cristina Kirchner is now more likely to stand for reelection (but without the valuable contribution and political coattails of a seasoned strategist) and a cabinet reshuffle over the next few months should not be ruled out," it says. "In addition, today's developments increase the probably that Daniel Scioli, current Governor of Buenos Aires Province, launches his own bid for the presidency competing for the same political space occupied by the Kirchners."\par \par \uc2 (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1432 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar bloc, including AUD, CAD and NZD, is piling up losses against the USD as US stocks hit intraday lows, and oil and other commodities sink. AUD/USD weighed down particularly by weaker-than-expected inflation data, with Aussie sinking nearly 2% against USD, dragging the other commodity-linked dollar-bloc \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down with it. USD/CAD up nearly 1%, while NZD/USD down nearly 0.9%. DJIA down 1%. (bradley.davis@dowjones.com) [ 