\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Chile to hike rate 25 bps in Oct- \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  poll\b0\par\par\uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 13:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Rate increase seen smallest in latest tightening cycle\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate seen at 3.25 percent by year-end\par \par \uc2 * Peso seen easing to 490 per dollar in December (Recasts with slowed tempo of hikes, adds analyst comment)\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Oct 13 (Reuters) - Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen slowing the pace of interest rate hikes, raising its benchmark rate by just 25 basis points later this week, as lower inflation expectations allow it to focus on stemming the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent strong rise.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll released on Wednesday showed financial analysts expect a 25-basis-point increase to 2.75 percent at the bank's Thursday monetary policy meeting, after four consecutive months of 50-basis-point hikes.\par \par \uc2 The rate hikes have been aimed at easing monetary stimulus after the economy showed clear signs of recovering from the global financial crisis and a massive Feb. 27 earthquake.\par \par \uc2 But with foreign capital pouring into the region given low rates elsewhere in the world, which has helped boost the peso nearly 15 percent since late June, the bank is unlikely to want to spur more inflows by boosting rates sharply.\par \par \uc2 The peso has also been pushed higher by the almost relentless appreciation in the price of copper, Chile's main export, and the weakness of the greenback.\par \par \uc2 "The fact the inflation expectations for year-end 2011 and longer horizons are relatively well anchored to the 3 percent target midpoint ... offers the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  extra degrees of freedom to calibrate the rate normalization cycle," Alberto Ramos, a senior economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , wrote in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected the bank's key rate to reach 3.25 percent in December and saw the peso <CLP=CL> easing 3 percent to 490 by the end of the year.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weighed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the peso's sharp appreciation for some time. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jose De Gregorio said this month the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was approaching the stronger end of levels justified by economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 The bank's last major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in 2008, after the peso firmed sharply amid a commodity-led boom. It gave the market a clear calendar outlining plans to buy $8 billion via auction.\par \par \uc2 September's IMACEC indicator of economic activity was seen growing by 7.0 percent from a year prior. (Reporting by Santiago newsroom; Writing by Brad Haynes and Hilary Burke; Editing by James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 CHILE-CENBANK/POLL (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABN|E|U|RBN|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101013e6ad000sk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
