\par\par \b \uc2 Chile De Gregorio:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Will Continue To Monitor Peso\b0\par\par\uc2 229 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 22:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to closely monitor the peso's exchange rate with the dollar, Valor Futuro newswire quoted bank president Jose De Gregorio as saying Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 De Gregorio, along with the other four \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors and Finance Minister Felipe Larrain, met with President Sebastian Pinera to discuss the peso's strength versus the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The peso has been trading in recent weeks at two-year highs, which has moved exporters to clamor for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy is highly dependent on its exports and a strong peso makes these products less competitive abroad.\par \par \uc2 De Gregorio noted that there was "good coordination" with the Finance Ministry, Valor Futuro reported.\par \par \uc2 The finance minister attends the bank's monthly monetary policy meetings and can voice an opinion, but he can't vote on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The central banker said the Tuesday meeting with Pinera and Larrain was part of the bank's role as an "adviser" to the president.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Pinera expressed his concern that the strong peso could slow down the agricultural sector, especially as the southern hemisphere's summer, and its export season, is fast approaching.\par \par \uc2 -By Carolina Pica, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8919; carolina.pica@dowjones.com [ 05-10-10 2040GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101005e6a5001b