\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Peso Ends At 27-Mo High;Exporters Want  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2010\par 23:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("\b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  Peso Ends At 27-Month High; Exporters Want \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," at 19:13 GMT, misstated the period during which the peso gained 8.8%, in the sixth paragraph. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Anthony Esposito \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso ended at a 27-month high against the dollar Thursday as international copper prices held at a two-year high, sparking a new round of exporters' demands for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The peso finished at CLP483.50 to the dollar, compared with CLP485.50 the prior session, while trading in a range of CLP482.70 to CLP485.00.\par \par \uc2 With Chile producing more than one-third of the world's copper, the peso often takes cues from the metal's international prices. Copper prices held at the two-year high reached following a four-month rally driven by improving industrial demand and concerns about supply.\par \par \uc2 As the strength of the peso cuts into the competitiveness of Chilean exports and because the local economy is highly dependent on its exports, exporters are turning up the heat as they demand market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, Chilean agricultural workers threatened general protests if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the peso market.\par \par \uc2 During the third quarter, the peso gained 8.8% on the dollar while, for the year, it has strengthened a milder 4.6% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Although in Latin America, Brazil, Colombia and Peru have already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tamp down their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the dollar's broad international weakness, the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market unless the peso strengthens past CLP460 or CLP470.\par \par \uc2 "While in nominal terms the exchange rate is concerning exporters, the real exchange rate is still considerably above levels that would merit \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader.\par \par \uc2 The last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, in April 2008, the peso was trading at 10-year highs against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed as institutional investors sold bonds, triggering stop-loss orders, a local fixed-income trader said.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended unchanged at 2.73%, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed higher at 3.08%, from 3.03% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 30-09-10 2101GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100930e69u001k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
