\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: BATS Still Sees Room To Run In Stock Trading\b0\par\par\uc2 1,204 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 15:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 9:51 (Dow Jones) Electronic exchange operator BATS Global Markets looking at a range of new asset classes and geographies, but still sees room for growing its core stock-trading franchises in US and Europe, according to Ticonderoga. Management thinks BATS could grab 18% of US cash equities market long term, vs current 10.2%, while its slice of the European pie could treble in size to 15% as London-based unit seen achieving profitability next year. Meanwhile, the company's weighing push into \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and futures, as well as potential stock platforms in Canada and Brazil. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:46 (Dow Jones) \uc2 AT&T\uc2  (T) put more than $275M to work upgrading its wireless network in the New York area during 1H, nearly eclipsing the $290M spent all of last year. T says the result is faster and better wireless in an area hampered by criticism over dropped calls and shoddy reception, partly due to a network overloaded with data-hungry Apple (AAPL) iPhones. T up 1% at $27.65. (paul.ziobro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:41 (Dow Jones) National Bank Financial suggests four "technical" reasons to take profits in Biovail (BVF), which is up 16% this month alone on higher-than-expected synergies from its merger with Valeant (VRX). Firm says the "flag breakout" at $19 fulfilled its upside objective of $24-$25; yesterday's reversal saw the stock close near its intraday lows after making "new rally highs"; at prices 25% above its 50-day average and 58% above its 200-day average, the stock is "extended"; and the stock turned back after approaching "chart resistance" around $27, indicating an unfavorable risk/reward. BVF up a fraction at $26.62. (andy.georgiades@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:36 (Dow Jones) Chile's peso strengthens against the greenback after the president of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tempered speculation yesterday of imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Faced with clamoring exporters who say peso's relative strength vs USD is hurting the competitiveness of their products, Jose De Gregorio responded that Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in line with its fundamentals. "His comments made it very clear that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not looking to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is comfortable with where the peso is currently trading," said Rodrigo Sarria, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with local investment bank Celfin Capital. Peso recently at CLP494.90 vs CLP496.50 at yesterday's close. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:28 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2  is broadly upbeat on a handful of biotech names, starting coverage of four companies with a buy ratings, expecting strong demand to bolster revenue and valuations. \uc2 Acorda Therapeutics\uc2  (ACOR), \uc2 Amgen\uc2  (AMGN), Celgene (CELG) and \uc2 Human Genome Sciences\uc2  (HGSI) all rated buy. Meanwhile, Gilead (GILD) rated hold as Stifel calls shares "fairly priced"; takes "conservative view of Gilead's well-publicized 'patent cliff' issues." \uc2 Biogen\uc2  (BIIB) rated sell, as it "continues to be a commercially challenged organization," and faces competitive threats in the multiple sclerosis market from \uc2 Novartis\uc2  (NVS) and \uc2 Merck KGaA\uc2 . (katherine.wegert@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:21 (Dow Jones) \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  (MCD) shares fall 2.7% premarket to $74.05 after August same-store sales report doesn't quite live up to expectations. MCD global same-store sales rise 4.9% last month, short of 5.3% consensus, Janney says, with Europe the main culprit on meager 2.2% growth, with France called out for its weakness. Janney notes France lowered the value-added tax in July 2009 to 5.5% from 19.6%, so MCD no longer realizing year-over-year benefits from that change. MCD has warned that Europe's austerity measures could slow sales there. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  thinks slowdown is due to weaker promotions in Europe. (paul.ziobro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:06 (Dow Jones) Apple's (AAPL) less-than-forthcoming press release about relaxing restrictions put on app developers may be an indication that it's retreating in its battle against \uc2 Adobe Systems\uc2 ' (ADBE) flash technology, used to design Internet sites. According to AAPL's release, one of the sections relaxed was 3.3.1, which had required developers to use AAPL-approved programming languages. In addition, AAPL now emphasizes, "We are relaxing all restrictions on the development tools used to create iOS apps." AAPL officials unavailable for further comment. ADBE shares jump 9.4% premarket to $32.05; AAPL adds 0.9%. (george.stahl@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:54 (Dow Jones) In another sign of investor appetite for the volatility trade, the CBOE Futures Exchange says it plans to launch weekly options on futures tied to the VIX volatility index. Walk it back: This is an option on a future on a batch of options tied to the S&P 500. Contracts expire weekly and one option corresponds to a single future. The move is "another way for customers to manage volatility," CBOE President Edward Joyce says in a release. (brendan.conway@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:51 (Dow Jones) The rally in stock futures following drop in jobless claims means the DJIA will once again test the 200-day moving average, which currently comes in around 10449. DJIA closed yesterday at 10387 and futures up 50. Investors already know there's resistance there, since DJIA was capped right at that level the previous three sessions, so unless bulls can pass the next "test" with a close above the 200-day, a pullback is very likely. Then the next level to test becomes the 50-day moving average at 10299. If DJIA can close above 200-day, next resistance area is 10670-10720 area. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:48 (Dow Jones) Total unadjusted continuing benefit claims for jobless workers collecting more than one week from state, extended and emergency programs fell only slightly in Aug 21 week, to 9.60 million from 9.66 million in prior week. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:45 (Dow Jones) US exports expanded 1.8% to $153.33B in July, highest level since August 2008. But the mix shows where US manufacturers have dominance and where they're flagging. Exports of capital goods jumped by $2.26B, with commercial planes accounting for more than half the gain. Sales of industrial supplies, such as fuel oil and gold, increased $502M. However, sales abroad of automotive vehicles and parts fell $375M. Exports of food, feed and beverages went down by $39M, while consumer goods exports slipped $8M. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:35 (Dow Jones) US stock futures jump higher after initial weekly jobless take a bigger-than-expected tumble and July trade deficit was much narrower than expected. Initial claims fall 27K, though the prior week was revised 6K higher; continuing claims fell by a mere 2K. S&P futures up 8.40; Dow futures up 66. (john.shipman@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 09, 201