\par\par \b \uc2 SCENARIOS- \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  peso rally may prompt  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2010\par 18:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Aug 19 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up warnings to investors on Thursday that financial authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the peso, which has sharply appreciated.\par \par \uc2 The peso has gained more than 6 percent since July as investors snapped up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cash in on rising \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Fresh warnings from Finance Minister Felipe Larrain on Thursday helped weaken the peso by 0.7 percent to 505.20/502.70 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 President Sebastian Pinera has also warned his government will use every macroeconomic tool at its disposal to make the peso more competitive. A stronger peso increases costs for exporters in the world's top copper producer.\par \par \uc2 The following are some of the possible scenarios in the short term as the peso continues to appreciate:\par \par \uc2 CLIMB TO NEAR 480/DOLLAR - VERBAL WARNINGS\par \par \uc2 The government is already stepping up verbal warnings to ease the peso's upward trend.\par \par \uc2 Now, if the peso keeps appreciating to near 480-per-dollar levels, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could join in the verbal exchange to temper the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jose De Gregorio repeatedly warned of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late 2009 and early 2010 after the peso surged. His warnings helped the peso slide back later in 2010.\par \par \uc2 The bank and central government may issue debt to absorb excess liquidity and exert more influence over the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The government could also slow repatriation of dollars from copper-boom savings held abroad and an international debt sale aimed at helping fund reconstruction after a massive Feb. 27 earthquake, to ease upward pressure on the peso.\par \par \uc2 A stronger-than-expected economic recovery could give the government some breathing room.\par \par \uc2 BELOW 480/DOLLAR, CENBANK SEEN STEPPING IN\par \par \uc2 If the peso firms to below 480 per dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The local market expects any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be similar to that seen in 2008, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  built up reserves through a dollar purchase program after the peso hit 430 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 That \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversed gains in the peso, which rose to the highest level in over a decade in 2008, driven by strong prices for key export, copper.\par \par \uc2 This month De Gregorio voiced concerns about foreign capital flows following rising rates in emerging economies, saying countries should be "alert and thinking what implications that has for monetary policy."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could impose capital controls by boosting banks' dollar reserve requirements, although analysts see that as a more unlikely choice.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also slow the pace of future rate hikes to make the peso less attractive to foreign investors, though this is also seen as less probable.\par \par \uc2 The government could change hedging limits or investment requirements for local pension funds, big players in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, to increase their dollar holdings and temper the peso's gains.\par \par \uc2 PESO SLIDES ON EXTERNAL FACTORS\par \par \uc2 A stronger dollar driven by fears of a double-dip recession in the United States as well as volatility in copper prices could again weaken the peso and prevent direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In early 2010, the peso slid from around 488 per dollar to 550 in just over two weeks. Wider dollar strength and the expiration of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  global crisis-driven liquidity measure helped ease the peso in late January. (Reporting by Maria Jose Latorre and Alonso Soto; Editing by Simon Gardner and Todd Eastham)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CHILE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -PESO/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SCENARIOS)|LANGEN|Table|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100819e68j0015k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
