\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexican peso leads region's losses on US data\b0\par\par\uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2010\par 16:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real dips 0.4 pct, Mexican peso loses 1.4 pct\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  outperforms region over week on growth optimism\par \par \uc2 * Investors eye \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Samantha Pearson and Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO/MEXICO CITY, Aug 6 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso headed for its biggest daily loss in more than two months on Friday, leading the region's losses, after data showed twice as many U.S. jobs were lost last month than economists had forecast.\par \par \uc2 The report added to concerns that the United States is facing a sharp slowdown, which would reduce demand for Latin American exports and make jittery investors less likely to buy the region's higher-risk assets.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <MXN=> lost 1.44 percent to 12.7409 per dollar, hitting its weakest level for a week and was set to record its steepest daily percentage decline since June 4.\par \par \uc2 "The peso's weakness is due to the perception of a slowdown in the United States and the impact this could have on economic activity in Mexico," said Salvador Orozco, a debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Santander in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's fragile economic recovery from the financial crisis is particularly dependent on U.S. growth, since the country sends most of its exports across its northern border.\par \par \uc2 Slowing U.S. activity would also reduce the amount of money that Mexican workers in the United States send to their families back home.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso was also set to record the region's deepest weekly loss of 0.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRBY> weakened 0.4 percent to 1.759 reais per U.S. dollar on the local spot market on Friday, and was heading for a weekly loss of 0.28 percent.\par \par \uc2 Implicit warnings from Brazilian officials that they will prevent the real from strengthening much past its current value have kept investors cautious around the 1.75 real-per dollar level.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's finance minister, Guido Mantega, said on Friday that the government was "concerned" about the real's strength. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Finance Ministry don't want (the real) to strengthen further," he said.\par \par \uc2 ANDEAN REGION STORMS AHEAD\par \par \uc2 While the Chilean peso was flat at 514.50 on Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was set to gain 1.26 percent over the week.\par \par \uc2 The South American country's economy is recovering much faster than expected from the effects of a devastating earthquake earlier this year, recent data has shown.\par \par \uc2 Booming growth could also put more pressure on its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sharply raise the cost of borrowing.\par \par \uc2 Analysts have widely been expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to opt for a third straight monthly rate increase of 50 basis points when it meets on August 12, but interest rate futures show market expectations for the year-end are rising.\par \par \uc2 Higher interest rates would help boost the Chilean peso by attracting more yield-hungry foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 "What we have is an economy that's growing in an important way, and at the same time, we don't have any significant inflationary pressures," said Francisco Hazbun, a trader with the Tanner brokerage in Santiago.\par \par \uc2 The Colombian peso <COP=STFX> settled at its strongest closing level in two years on Friday, as investors continued to bet on inflows into the country's mining and energy sectors.\par \par \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <PEN=PE>hit a fresh two-year intraday low on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Peruvian economy grew more than 9 percent in the second quarter, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials said on Friday, a day after raising interest rates by more than expected to avert inflationary pressures. [ID:nN06260729]\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Molly Rosbach in Santiago; Editing by Paul Simao)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|D|E|RBN|M|U|RNP|DNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100809e68600170\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
