\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Chile watching fundamentals for peso-finmin\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn   \par \uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2009\par 12:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds background, growth comments)\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 17 (Reuters) - Chile is keeping a close eye on the fundamentals that may be affecting the peso, Chile's finance minister said on Thursday, as the country battles with a near 30 percent rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 "Underlying fundamentals matter. We keep a very close watch on those fundamentals," Andres Velasco told a news briefing.\par \par \uc2 "The level of the exchange rate and competitiveness of our exports is a key element in the country's performance and economic growth."\par \par \uc2 The peso <CLP=> has surged as foreign investors return to a pre-crisis hunt for yield, and there is an upturn in demand for copper, Chile's no. 1 export.\par \par \uc2 Other countries, including Brazil, have imposed capital controls to curb rise in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which hurts the competitiveness of exporters.\par \par \uc2 Velasco declined to say at what level he would like the peso to trade, or whether he would like to see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said that they do not rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  under extraordinary circumstances, I am not adding anything."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week maintained its outlook for 2010 economic growth in Chile of 4.5 to 5.5 percent, as the economy bounces back from its first recession in a decade.\par \par \uc2 The bank also forecast an economic contraction of 1.9 percent in 2009, compared with a previous outlook for a drop of 1.5 to 2.0 percent.\par \par \uc2 Velasco said the finance ministry's own 2009 growth prediction, in the budget submitted in September, was for a contraction of 1.6 percent, with a growth forecast for 2010 of 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Yesterday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast -1.9 percent (for 2009), it's only slightly worse than we had forecast," Velasco said.\par \par \uc2 "We have had four consecutive months of month-on-month expansion -- we are expecting a fourth quarter of even more rigorous growth."\par \par \uc2 Chile was invited this week to join the Organisation of Economic Cooperation and Development as its 31st member, the first country from South America to join the club of mostly wealthy free-market democracies.\par \par \uc2 Conservative billionaire Sebastian Pinera led Chile's weekend presidential vote and is seen the favourite to win a run-off and lead a political shift in Latin America's most stable economy, after 20 years of leftist rule.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Patrick Graham)\par \par \uc2 CHILE-PESO (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091217e5ch000tg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
