\par\par \b \uc2 Chile peso sinks on firm dollar, stocks up\b0\par\par\uc2 508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2009\par 22:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Nov 20 (Reuters) - Chile's peso <CLP=CL> ended slightly weaker on Friday, recovering from earlier steep losses as traders sold U.S. dollars to lock in profits, while stocks shot up in late trading on a brighter market outlook.\par \par \uc2 The peso fell 0.1 percent to 501.20/501.70 per dollar after falling sharply on wider dollar strength and global stock losses. The peso closed at 500.70/501.20 on Thursday, posting its biggest daily drop in nearly seven months. Earlier this week the peso had flirted with 17-month highs.\par \par \uc2 "The market overreacted in early trade, closing (dollar) positions motivated by a fairly negative external outlook and because the dollar had been strengthening against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days," said one trader.\par \par \uc2 "When the exchange rate hit 508 pesos per dollar, there was an increase in dollars offered due to profit-taking, which enabled the peso to recover almost all of the ground lost early in the day," he added.\par \par \uc2 Traders said a fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warning on Thursday of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb peso appreciation of around 27 percent against the dollar year-to-date added only marginal pressure to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose abroad on Friday, extending gains from the previous session as investors shied away from riskier assets, while murmurs of capital controls weighed on some emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Russia's rouble continued to retreat from 2009 highs on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric about possible measures and rate cuts to stem speculative capital flows, while Brazil's real continued to depreciate a day after weakening on concerns over a new government tax aimed at curbing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The peso has been one of the best-performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year against a weaker U.S. dollar and is still trading around 16-month highs. Some analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it hits 480 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 On stocks, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's blue-chip IPSA share index <.IPSA> pared early losses to close 0.77 percent higher at 3,289.60 points, on an imported market outlook, traders said. The all-market IGPA <.IGPA> rose 0.60 percent to 15,509.60.\par \par \uc2 "The market was slow until 3 p.m. and then investors began buying, I think on improved expectations for next week," said Pablo Matte, a trader with BICE brokerage. "In general, stocks have been punished over the last few days, investors saw the prices as attractive and started buying."\par \par \uc2 Conglomerate Copec , the most heavily weighted share on the Santiago bourse, surged 3.05 percent to 7,275 pesos.\par \par \uc2 Chile-based regional energy group \uc2 Enersis\uc2  , climbed 0.61 percent to 191 pesos.\par \par \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  , one of Latin America's largest retailers, rallied after several days of losses on speculation that Peru's Wong family could sell it's 2.28 percent stake in the company. \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  rose 0.79 percent to 1,510 pesos.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHILE/|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|M|D|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091120e5bk001n9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
