\par\par \b \uc2 LATAM WEEKAHEAD-Investors on guard for possible  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Walter Brandimarte   \par \uc2 491 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2009\par 18:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 15 (Reuters) - Latin American investors will be on the lookout for possible government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week, as the appreciation of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasingly threatens exporters.\par \par \uc2 Brazil could soon impose additional measures to curb the appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRBY>, as it nears the psychological level of 1.7 per U.S. dollar -- about the same level the real was trading when the government imposed a 2 percent financial tax on foreign inflows last month.\par \par \uc2 "We see very high odds that the authorities will introduce further measures aimed at curbing exchange rate appreciation," said Enrique Alvarez, Latin America strategist for IDEAglobal in New York, citing the transfer of the economist Emilio Garofalo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  specialist, from the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the Finance Ministry.\par \par \uc2 Garofalo's move, he said, was "viewed by many as preceding further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , both \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jose De Gregorio and Finance Minister Andres Velasco have warned that the government may act soon to tame the strength of the peso <CLP=CL>, which has been trading at 15-month highs. [ID:nN12419196]\par \par \uc2 Also on investors' radar will be the approval of Mexico's 2010 budget expenditures over the weekend and how ratings agencies will react to the final budget.\par \par \uc2 Any dilution of the government's plan to cut expenditures by 74.2 billion pesos, or 0.6 percent of gross domestic product, could have a critical impact on the agencies' decision on whether to downgrade Mexico's ratings, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 On the data front, Latin America will have a relatively light schedule this week. The following are some key data points that investors will watch:\par \par \uc2 Sunday Nov. 15\par \par \uc2 * Mexico - Deadline for the lower house of Congress to pass expenditure side of the 2010 budget.\par \par \uc2 Monday Nov. 16\par \par \uc2 * Mexico - Market holiday\par \par \uc2 * Brazil - Formal job creation in October: \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  forecasts that 75,000 jobs were created, following 252,617 new jobs in the previous month.\par \par \uc2 "The forecast is compatible with job creation losing steam in the margin. If the forecast is right, year to date, slightly more than 1 million jobs have been added to the economy," the bank said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday Nov. 18\par \par \uc2 * Chile - Third-quarter GDP: Expected to shrink 1.3 percent year-on-year, following a 4.5 percent contraction in the previous period.\par \par \uc2 Friday Nov. 20\par \par \uc2 * Brazil - Market holiday\par \par \uc2 * Mexico - Third-quarter GDP: expected to shrink 6.2 percent year-on-year, after a 6.9 percent contraction in the previous period.\par \par \uc2 * Colombia - Rate decision: Most analysts expect the bank to hold interest rates at 4.0 percent, without ruling out a cut to 3.5 percent as the economy remains on a disinflationary path. (Editing by Jan Paschal)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING/WEEKAHEAD|LANGEN|ABN|E|U|M|D|RBN|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091118e5bf00170\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
