\par\par \b \uc2 Chile ups public sector wages, peso seen easing\b0\par\par\uc2 302 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2009\par 15:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Nov 12 (Reuters) - Chile's Senate approved a 4.5 percent salary hike for around 500,000 public sector employees late on Wednesday, a move analysts said should help ease pressure on a sharply appreciating peso.\par \par \uc2 Public sector employees have staged a series of strikes and protests in recent weeks to demand higher wages.\par \par \uc2 Local media reported the 4.5 percent hike ranks as the largest increase in two decades, after factoring inflation data.\par \par \uc2 On an annual basis, the consumer price index fell 1.9 percent in the 12 months through October, the National Statistics Institute said last week.\par \par \uc2 The civil servants' wage hike comes as Chile's peso is trading at 15 month highs after surging around 26 percent year-to-date, and as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has warned it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "In \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's case, there have been no red lights or anything to suggest there is fiscal disorder," said Pedro Tuesta, senior analyst with market research company 4CAST in Washington D.C.\par \par \uc2 "Increased government spending usually generates greater aggregate demand, and that generates more demand for imports," he added. "We know the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are worried about the exchange rate and an increase in aggregate demand will always help halt the appreciation of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The peso <CLP=CL> closed at 507.00/507.50 per U.S. dollar on Wednesday, the strongest close since August 2008, though was slightly weaker in early Thursday trade.\par \par \uc2 The peso's sharp gains year-to-date have more than reversed a 22.3 percent slide in 2008 amid the worst of the global financial crisis. (Writing by Simon Gardner, editing by W Simon )\par \par \uc2 CHILE-ECONOMY/WAGES|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|RNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091112e5bc001n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
